Self-Assessment: The Label Matrix Representation in Snorkel <p>In Snorkel weak supervision, explain the structure of the Label Matrix \(\mathbf{\Lambda} \in \{-1, 0, 1\}^{n 	imes m}\) for a binary classification task with \(n\) instances and \(m\) labeling functions.</p> Reveal Detailed Solution <p>Answer: Each entry \(\Lambda_{i, j}\) represents the output of labeling function \(\lambda_j\) on data instance \(x_i\): - \(\Lambda_{i, j} = +1\): The rule votes for the positive class. - \(\Lambda_{i, j} = -1\): The rule votes for the negative class. - \(\Lambda_{i, j} = 0\): The rule abstains (the pattern was not detected; the rule provides zero opinion). The matrix is sparse because each heuristic specializes in a specific sub-pattern and abstains on the vast majority of records.</p> Self-Assessment: Coverage vs. Conflict Rate in Weak Supervision <p>Define Coverage and Conflict Rate of a suite of labeling functions. What does a high conflict rate signal?</p> Reveal Detailed Solution <p>Answer: - Coverage: The fraction of total instances in the dataset where at least one labeling function does not abstain: \( rac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{I}(\exists j : \Lambda_{i, j} 
